[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the buildable one.

  Now the decomposition. Temporal notifications need tasks with due dates, and neither exists. So this is two
  sub-projects, and only the second is the thing you asked for:

  1. [1mMinimal task layer[0m — a task model (title, due date, done), [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m persistence, and enough UI to create and
     complete tasks. Exists solely so notifications have something to fire on.
  2. [1mNotification system[0m — trigger evaluation (due soon / overdue / stale), a scheduler that runs while the page is
     open, delivery (in-app + browser Notification API), and dismissal/snooze state so you aren't re-nagged.

  Sub-project 1 is small but it is not free, and its shape constrains sub-project 2 — a task with no [38;5;153mdue[39m field supports
  no due-date trigger, a task with no [38;5;153mupdatedAt[39m supports no staleness trigger. So I need to know how much of it you want
  before I can spec the part you care about.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much task layer should this build include? → Bare minimum (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two more rounds of questions, then I'll present the design.

  Triggers are the heart of it. Each one costs a field in the model and a rule in the evaluator, so I'd rather ship two
  that work well than five that half-fire.
